  <header class="site-header"><div class="container nav-wrap"><a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark"></span><span>Test Before You Build</span></a><nav class="main-nav" aria-label="Primary navigation"><a href="/">Homepage</a><a href="/market-validation-services-pricing/">Services and pricing</a><a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">About Dr. Kari</a></nav><a class="button button-small" href="/#contact">Submit your idea</a></div></header>
  <main><article>
    <header class="article-hero"><div class="container article-head"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Test Before You Build</a><span>›</span><span>B2B customer research</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Participant recruitment guide</p><h1>B2B customer research: how to find and recruit the right interview participants</h1><p class="standfirst">Useful B2B customer research starts with the people whose experience, authority or constraints matter to the decision. Define those people precisely, build a defensible prospect list and recruit participants without confusing convenience, seniority or enthusiasm with relevance.</p><div class="byline"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="72" height="72" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen" /><div><strong>By <a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</a></strong><span>Market validation, commercialisation and customer discovery</span><time datetime={published}>Published and updated 17 September 2026</time></div></div><aside class="scope-summary"><strong>The short answer</strong><p>Define the decision first. Translate it into participant criteria, separate buyer and user roles, identify more suitable prospects than the required interview total, approve the list before outreach and track who accepts so the final sample still reflects the intended market.</p></aside></div></header>
    <div class="container article-layout"><aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#why">Why recruitment matters</a><a href="#definition">Define participants</a><a href="#find">Where to find them</a><a href="#recruit">Recruitment process</a><a href="#outreach">Outreach</a><a href="#quality">Sample quality</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></aside><div class="article-body">
      <section id="why"><h2>Participant recruitment determines the value of B2B customer research</h2><p>An excellent interview with an irrelevant participant produces precise information about the wrong market. Recruitment is therefore part of the research design—not an administrative step after the questions have been written.</p><p>B2B buying decisions may involve users, managers, budget holders, technical evaluators, procurement teams, compliance specialists and external partners. Each sees a different part of the problem. The correct participants depend on the assumption being tested.</p><blockquote>The question is not “Who can we persuade to speak with us?” It is “Whose experience or authority can change this decision?”</blockquote><h3>Purposeful selection is not statistical sampling</h3><p>Customer discovery interviews normally use purposeful selection: participants are chosen because they have relevant experience, roles or circumstances. The aim is depth and decision relevance, not a statistically representative estimate of an entire market.</p><p>This makes selection criteria especially important. A larger convenience sample does not repair a mismatch between the research question and the people interviewed.</p></section>
      <section id="definition"><h2>Define the right B2B interview participants</h2><p>Begin with the commercial decision and map the people involved. Write inclusion and exclusion criteria before searching for names.</p><div class="factor-grid"><div><h3>Organisation</h3><p>Industry, size, geography, business model, technology environment or regulatory setting.</p></div><div><h3>Role</h3><p>User, manager, economic buyer, technical evaluator, procurement professional, partner or other stakeholder.</p></div><div><h3>Experience</h3><p>Recent exposure to the problem, workflow, purchase, implementation or failed alternative being studied.</p></div><div><h3>Situation</h3><p>A trigger, constraint, growth stage, current solution or consequence that makes the question relevant.</p></div><div><h3>Authority</h3><p>First-hand knowledge of use, influence over requirements or responsibility for budget and approval.</p></div><div><h3>Exclusions</h3><p>Conflicts, missing experience, unsuitable markets or relationships likely to distort the evidence.</p></div></div>
      <h3>Separate users, buyers and decision-makers</h3><p>A user may experience the problem without controlling the budget. A buyer may approve a purchase without using the product. IT, procurement or compliance may block adoption despite support from both. Recruit and analyse these roles separately when their incentives differ.</p><h3>Use screening questions</h3><p>Ask short factual questions that verify role, recent experience, current approach and involvement in the decision. Avoid screening solely on interest in the proposed idea; enthusiasm is not evidence of relevance.</p></section>
      <aside class="inline-cta"><p class="eyebrow">Need the right people, not convenient people?</p><h2>Independent recruitment starts with an agreed target definition.</h2><p>For the standard service, Dr. Kari identifies approximately 30–40 suitable prospects. The client approves the list before outreach begins, and Dr. Kari recruits and interviews 20 participants.</p><a class="button" href="/market-validation-services-pricing/">See the validation process</a></aside>
      <section id="find"><h2>Where to find B2B interview participants</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Risk to manage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Company websites and team pages</td><td>Target organisations and roles are identifiable</td><td>Titles may not reveal actual responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Professional networks</td><td>Roles, careers and company context can be screened</td><td>Connection does not establish relevance or consent</td></tr><tr><td>Industry directories and associations</td><td>The market has recognised categories or membership</td><td>Lists may be incomplete or commercially biased</td></tr><tr><td>Conference programmes and specialist events</td><td>Speakers and participants demonstrate domain involvement</td><td>Visible experts may not represent ordinary buyers or users</td></tr><tr><td>Partner and supplier ecosystems</td><td>Market access depends on intermediaries or complementary products</td><td>Referrers may select people who share their views</td></tr><tr><td>Existing customers and contacts</td><td>Adoption, implementation or retention is being studied</td><td>The sample may overrepresent supporters and current relationships</td></tr><tr><td>Referrals and snowball recruitment</td><td>The group is specialist or difficult to identify directly</td><td>Networks can create clusters of similar participants</td></tr><tr><td>Research panels or recruiters</td><td>Speed, volume or specialist screening justifies external support</td><td>Verify identity, screening quality and incentive effects</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use several sources when one source would overrepresent a particular network, company type or point of view. Keep a prospect register showing why each person qualifies and which customer group they represent.</p></section>
      <section id="recruit"><h2>A practical B2B participant recruitment process</h2><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Define the decision and participant groups.</strong><span>State whose evidence is needed and why.</span></li><li><strong>Set quotas or coverage goals where necessary.</strong><span>Protect important roles, segments or situations from disappearing as recruitment progresses.</span></li><li><strong>Create inclusion and exclusion criteria.</strong><span>Use observable qualifications rather than vague labels such as “innovative company.”</span></li><li><strong>Build a prospect list larger than the interview target.</strong><span>Not everyone will reply, qualify or be available.</span></li><li><strong>Review and approve the list.</strong><span>Resolve disputed companies, roles, conflicts and missing groups before outreach.</span></li><li><strong>Contact prospects in controlled waves.</strong><span>Monitor acceptance by segment so early convenience does not determine the final sample.</span></li><li><strong>Screen and schedule participants.</strong><span>Confirm factual eligibility, purpose, duration, confidentiality and any recording arrangements.</span></li><li><strong>Track recruitment outcomes.</strong><span>Record contacted, replied, qualified, declined, scheduled, completed and replaced participants.</span></li><li><strong>Adjust deliberately.</strong><span>If one group is inaccessible, document the limitation or revise the design instead of silently substituting easier participants.</span></li></ol><p><a href="/customer-discovery-interviews/">Read the customer discovery interview questions, process and evidence guide</a>.</p><p><a href="/how-many-customer-interviews/">Read how participant relevance and segment coverage affect the number of interviews needed</a>.</p></section>
      <section id="outreach"><h2>Write outreach that earns a response without turning into a sales pitch</h2><p>A good invitation is brief and specific. It should explain who is conducting the research, why the person appears relevant, what the discussion concerns, how long it takes, how information will be used and whether the interview will be recorded.</p><div class="message"><p><strong>Subject:</strong> Research interview about [specific workflow or problem]</p><p>Hello [Name],</p><p>I am conducting independent research into how [defined role or organisation] currently handles [specific situation]. Your experience with [relevant factual context] appears relevant.</p><p>This is a research interview, not a sales call. The discussion would take approximately [duration] and focus on your current process, alternatives and decision criteria. I will explain how the information and any recording will be handled before the interview.</p><p>Would you be open to taking part?</p></div><p>Personalisation should establish relevance, not simulate familiarity. Follow applicable privacy, direct-marketing and organisational rules. Avoid excessive follow-up and make declining easy.</p><h3>Should incentives be offered?</h3><p>Compensation may be appropriate for specialist participants or substantial time commitments. Decide it consistently and disclose it clearly. An incentive compensates time; it does not convert an unqualified respondent into a useful participant.</p></section>
      <section id="quality"><h2>Protect sample quality while recruitment is underway</h2><ul><li>Do not fill the sample with the first people who reply.</li><li>Do not replace senior buyers with junior users when the research question concerns budget authority.</li><li>Do not rely only on existing customers, friendly partners or the founder’s network.</li><li>Do not merge distinct roles simply to report a larger total.</li><li>Do not allow one organisation to dominate a market-wide study without a stated reason.</li><li>Do not hide inaccessible groups; report what their absence means for the conclusion.</li></ul><h3>When the target group is difficult to reach</h3><p>Improve the target definition, use credible introductions, offer flexible scheduling, shorten the requested commitment or recruit through specialist channels. If the intended buyers remain unreachable, that may itself be commercially relevant: the future sales process may face the same access problem.</p><h3>Why client approval matters</h3><p>The research provider understands selection logic; the client often knows conflicts, existing relationships and market context. Reviewing the prospect list together catches errors before outreach while preserving the interviewer’s independence during data collection and analysis.</p></section>
      <section id="faq"><p class="eyebrow">Frequently asked questions</p><h2>Questions about B2B research recruitment</h2><div class="faq-list">{faqs.map(({question,answer})=><details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div></section>
      <section class="final-cta"><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Independent B2B customer research</p><h2>Reach the people whose evidence matters to the decision.</h2><p>The standard project includes target definition, approximately 30–40 prospects for client approval, recruitment, 20 interviews, transcripts, evidence analysis, a written report, recommended changes and a BUILD, CHANGE or KILL recommendation.</p><p>Fixed price: €2,900 + VAT. Typical timeline: approximately 2–4 weeks. Larger and multi-segment projects can be scoped separately.</p><a class="button button-light" href="/#contact">Submit your idea</a></section>
      <section class="author-card"><img src="/kari-jaaskelainen.jpg" width="160" height="160" alt="Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen" loading="lazy" /><div><p class="eyebrow">About the author</p><h2>Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen</h2><p>Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen is an entrepreneur, business leader and consultant with experience in market validation, technology commercialisation, business development and sales. He has worked on around 200 commercialisation and consulting projects and has made approximately 2,000 sales calls a year.</p><a href="/about-kari-jaaskelainen/">Read about Dr. Kari Jääskeläinen’s experience →</a></div></section>
      <section class="sources"><h2>Sources and further reading</h2><ol><li>Harvard Business School Rock Center for Entrepreneurship. <a href="https://entrepreneurship.hbs.edu/Documents/Session%20Summary/HBSRock-Customer-Discovery-Final.pdf"><cite>Customer Discovery Basics</cite></a>.</li><li>Palinkas et al. (2015). <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4012002/"><cite>Purposeful sampling for qualitative data collection and analysis in mixed method implementation research</cite></a>.</li><li>DeJonckheere and Vaughn (2019). <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6910737/"><cite>Semistructured interviewing in primary care research</cite></a>.</li><li>U.S. National Science Foundation. <a href="https://www.nsf.gov/funding/initiatives/i-corps"><cite>NSF I-Corps</cite></a>.</li></ol></section>
